{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1791934111999,"sessionID":"ses_ec869172affeZ2EyFY2MELrMQp","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-a2174de88478c492","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"git commit -m \"feat(distribute): fence the top-level opencode session (ADR-2027)\" -m \"P07 run 5: launching the session as the subagent fence left the parent with its own tools, so a command's tool fence guarded only the subagent, not the top-level session. Flipping the fence agents to mode:all and resolving the command's fence agent in run() puts the whole session inside the fence; a command naming a missing or mismatched fence is refused (NO-FENCE) rather than run unfenced. The harness-proof header now records the fence each session ran under (or explicit override / none).\""},"output":"[feat/distribute-c3 1993cb6b] feat(distribute): fence the top-level opencode session (ADR-2027)\n 27 files changed, 80 insertions(+), 24 deletions(-)\n create mode 100644 initiatives/distribute/evidence/phase-08/transcripts/kill-arc-resume.jsonl\n create mode 100644 initiatives/distribute/evidence/phase-08/transcripts/kill-arc-resume.jsonl.stderr\n create mode 100644 initiatives/distribute/evidence/phase-08/transcripts/probe-fence.jsonl\n create mode 100644 initiatives/distribute/evidence/phase-08/transcripts/probe-fence.jsonl.stderr\n","metadata":{"output":"[feat/distribute-c3 1993cb6b] feat(distribute): fence the top-level opencode session (ADR-2027)\n 27 files changed, 80 insertions(+), 24 deletions(-)\n create mode 100644 initiatives/distribute/evidence/phase-08/transcripts/kill-arc-resume.jsonl\n create mode 100644 initiatives/distribute/evidence/phase-08/transcripts/kill-arc-resume.jsonl.stderr\n create mode 100644 initiatives/distribute/evidence/phase-08/transcripts/probe-fence.jsonl\n create mode 100644 initiatives/distribute/evidence/phase-08/transcripts/probe-fence.jsonl.stderr\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"git commit -m \"feat(distribute): fence the top-level opencode session (ADR-2027)\" -m \"P07 run 5: launching the session as the subagent fence left the parent with its own tools, so a command's tool fence guarded only the subagent, not the top-level session. Flipping the fence agents to mode:all and resolving the command's fence agent in run() puts the whole session inside the fence; a command naming a missing or mismatched fence is refused (NO-FENCE) rather than run unfenced. The harness-proof header now records the fence each session ran under (or explicit override / none).\"","time":{"start":1791934111967,"end":1791934111980}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The file is still being modified even after re-staging. There's an active writer appending to `probe-fence.jsonl`. This is a live transcript — likely a background process from the phase-08 probe still running, or something in this harness writing.\n\nCommitting a file that's actively being written has two problems:\n1. The staged copy is a snapshot; the commit will capture whatever was staged at commit time — actually, when I run `git commit`, it commits the index (staged) content, not the working tree. So whatever I last staged is what gets committed. The unstaged modification won't be included. So the commit wouldn't be \"torn\" — it'd just be a slightly older snapshot of the transcript.\n2. But leaving unstaged changes means a dirty working tree after commit.\n\nOptions:\na) Commit now with the staged snapshot, and leave the ongoing writer's new content unstaged. Then note that probe-fence.jsonl is still being written and will need a follow-up commit. This keeps the commit clean of a torn line.\nb) Wait for it to settle — but I can't easily wait/poll without consuming turns, and I don't know how long the writer runs.\n\nGiven this is a live transcript being actively appended (probably by a lingering probe process), the pragmatic arc-commit behavior: commit the stable staged snapshot now. A transcript that's mid-write gets a consistent snapshot from the index. The ongoing writer's tail will show as unstaged, which I'll report honestly.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider whether this is the right call. The evidence files — is committing a partial transcript harmful? For evidence purposes, a partial transcript is still evidence of the probe. The alternative is blocking indefinitely on a writer I can't control. The instruction says \"Never commit blind\" — I understand exactly what's in the staged snapshot (the probe-fence transcript up to the point I staged it). That's not blind.\n\nI'll proceed with the commit using the staged snapshot. I'll report that probe-fence.jsonl had ongoing writes and its latest tail remains unstaged.\n\nLet me write the commit message.\n\nSubject (≤72 chars): `feat(distribute): fence the top-level opencode session (ADR-2027)`\nCount: \"feat(distribute): \" = 18, \"fence the top-level opencode session\" = 36, \" (ADR-2027)\" = 11 → 65 chars. Good.\n\nBody (why):\n- P07 run 5: launching the session as a subagent fence left the parent process with its own tools, so a command's tool fence protected only the subagent, not the top-level session.\n- Flipping the fence agents to mode:all and resolving the command's fence agent in run() puts the whole session inside the fence.\n- A command naming a missing/mismatched fence is refused NO-FENCE rather than run unfenced.\n- harness-proof header records the fence (or none / explicit override) for audit.\n\nI'll use `git commit -m \"...\" -m \"...\"`. Careful about characters: body has no single quotes, no backticks, no `$`. It has colons and parentheses — fine inside double quotes. Use double quotes for the -m args.\n\nActually, the body lines in -m: each -m becomes a paragraph. I'll do one -m for subject and one -m for body with newlines? Multi-line -m strings with embedded newlines inside double quotes work in bash. Let me write:\n\ngit commit -m \"feat(distribute): fence the top-level opencode session (ADR-2027)\" -m \"P07 run 5: launching the session as the subagent fence left the parent with its own tools, so a command's tool fence guarded only the subagent, not the top-level session. Flipping the fence agents to mode:all and resolving the command's fence agent in run() puts the whole session inside the fence; a command naming a missing or mismatched fence is refused (NO-FENCE) rather than run unfenced. The harness-proof header now records the fence each session ran under (or explicit override / none).\"\n\nThat's a long body but fine. No single quotes, no backticks, no $. Good.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_137986b6c001W39MZkoPBJIHFP","sessionID":"ses_ec869172affeZ2EyFY2MELrMQp","messageID":"msg_1379849010014ewyf4wvQsiML4"}}
